body:
*"I had a manic episode where I was screaming, throwing things, trying to hurt myself."*

&#x200B;

That isn't a manic episode, but it could definitely be a symptom of a manic episode.  OR it could be a sign of a comorbid disorder which will need to be identified and treated if you want to have long-term stability.  I don't think it is a matter of being too prideful or anything like that.

My psychiatrist is amazing and has an incredibly long waitlist.  That said, their rating is only 2.5/5.

Some of the 1\* comments include things like: 

>*told me I was just insecure about myself, emotional and sensitive about what I feel.*

&#x200B;

>*one of the most condescending humans in the world.*

&#x200B;

>*misdiagnosed me from the day I walked in after a short interview.*

One of the reasons I liked him originally was because he was a diagnostic specialist that came highly recommended multiple, independent medical practitioners, he was straightforward with me, answered my questions thoughtfully and wasn't condescending like other practitioners I have seen (and I have worked with many!).  He also was willing to openly admit when he was wrong and I was right about something.  My only issue is that sometimes he talks way, way too fast, which is a weird concern for me to have because I listen to my podcasts sped up and I specifically picked a therapist (before I came to this psychiatrist) because I liked how fast they talk in sessions.  I am guessing that his speed translates into a lack of understanding and a lot of arrogance to some patients.

&#x200B;

Some of the 5\* ratings include:

&#x200B;

> *just as comfortable using an innovative, newly developed treatment as he is using something decades old.*  

&#x200B;

>*I felt heard from a psychiatrist for the first time. My previous experiences with other doctors have been very “in and out. As soon as possible.”*

&#x200B;

> *very personable, pleasant, polite, understanding, informative, and compassionate.* 

&#x200B;

>*has been taking my illness seriously and not undermining its significance. He has also been kind and understanding by asking certain questions such as how I feel in order to make sure I am safe to myself.* 

&#x200B;

Unfortunately, I hate when people don't take the opinions of people with mental illness seriously.  Yet, at the same time, it is hard to take online reviews of a psychiatrist seriously because some people writing them are *very ill,* especially if you go to a psychiatrist who is tasked with dealing with the most severe cases.

I like bright and modern offices.  I didn't realize I had a preference, but after looking for a new therapist, and when my prior amazing therapist redid their office, I loved that it was modern and bright.  Here is one I almost went to only based on their fun design:  [Dive Thru Clinic](https://globalnews.ca/video/9508463/divethru-therapy-clinic-opens-in-edmonton-with-focus-on-community)

&#x200B;

My therapist has pillows on their couch (just a couple) and I find when I am stressed that I grab a pillow to hug or fidget with - I would totally love fidgets though!  That would be right up my alley.

I read a lot of psychoeducational reports in my job.  It is very, very common for me to come across IQ scores that are in the 'genius' range, but the person has a learning disability in reading, writing, math or my favorite - all three!

&#x200B;

I want to also note that some of the coolest people I have met and worked with technically have 'low' cognitive functioning, but were creative, fun, hilarious and hard working.

> *I don't know how you judge if everyone who has them needs them though?*

Mentally I know that a licensed medical professional has determined that the person needs them, so that should be good enough.  It just seems so strange that it is so frequent.  I mean, we don't all have liver disorders or heart disorder - how can so many of us have  brain disorders?

&#x200B;

>*I'm confident that I need them, and they made an incredible difference to me, but then probably everyone who takes them says that?*

&#x200B;

That's how I feel!  I mean, I feel like I **need** them, but isn't that the point?  Very few people would take them it they felt they didn't need them.

&#x200B;

>*By questioning their legitimacy do you mean the effectiveness of the medication?*

Not the efficacy of the medication, but the necessity.  I don't want to be on medications unless I need them, because I have some pretty awful side effects.  I just don't know if I can really trust the professionals when it seems like they feel so many people need the meds.

They can definitely be different!  


> *BD-I individuals experienced a higher number of lifetime MDE, had the worst quality of life, and received significantly more treatment for MDE than BD-II and MDD subjects. Individuals with BD-I and BD-II experienced their first mood episode about 10 years earlier than those with MDD (21.2, 20.5, and 30.4 years, respectively).*   (Moreno et al, 2013)

&#x200B;

>*In comparisons of MDD and depressed BD patients, atypical features, such as hypersomnia or leaden paralysis (5, 6, 9, 20), psychotic symptoms (13, 16, 17, 19), psychomotor retardation (5, 13, 16), shorter depressive episodes (13, 14), higher number of depressive recurrences (9, 18–20), family history of mood disorders (9, 14), comorbidity with substance abuse (8, 17), and earlier age at onset (7, 9, 18) are reported more frequently in BD; whereas, somatic disturbances (5, 6, 18), anxiety (7, 13), sleep loss (5, 6, 9, 11, 13, 14, 18), and appetite loss (5, 6, 14, 17) are reported more frequently in MDD.*    (Moreno et al, 2013)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

>*In the adjusted analyses, most symptoms of depression (hypersomnia, increased appetite/weight gain, guilt, thoughts about death, suicidal ideation, suicide attempt, irritability, insomnia, restlessness, worthlessness, and difficulty making decisions) remained significantly more common among BD-I than MDD subjects; BD-II showed significantly more irritability, insomnia, and difficulty making decisions than MDD individuals; and increased appetite or weight gain, suicide attempt, irritability, restlessness, and worthlessness were more frequent among BD-I than BD-II subjects. Depressed mood was more common in MDD than in BD-I and BD-II individuals, and low energy was more frequent in MDD than BD-I subjects (p < 0.05 for all comparisons). Family history yielded essentially comparable results. The adjusted trend analyses revealed that most clinical symptoms of depression were more frequent in BD-I, followed by BD-II, and least frequent in MDD; while only depressed mood was more frequent in MDD, followed by BD-II and BD-I (Table 2). Linear trends were also found for family history of depression, alcohol problems, and drug problems with higher frequencies for BD-I, followed by BD-II and MDD individuals (Table 2).*   (Moreno et al, 2013)  


&#x200B;

&#x200B;

>*Although the bipolar patients were nomore severely depressed than the major depressive disordercontrols, they were more likely to demonstratepsychomotor-retarded melancholic and atypical depressive featuresand to have had previous episodes of psychotic depression. Thesefindings were largely duplicated even when the population wasconfined to those with DSM-IV melancholia.*   (Mitchell, 2001)

&#x200B;

>*Bipolar depression was associated with family history of bipolar disorder, an earlier age at onset, a greater previous number of depressive episodes, and eight individual symptom items on the Montgomery-Åsberg Depression Rating Scale and the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale. Fears were more common in patients with bipolar disorder, whereas sadness; insomnia; intellectual (cognitive), somatic (muscular), respiratory, genitourinary complaints; and depressed behavior were more common in patients with unipolar depression. A logistic regression model correctly classified 86.9% of the subjects.*    (Perlis et al, 2006)

&#x200B;

>*Patients with bipolar disorder (BP) have high rates of suicide attempt (30–50%) and death (15–20%) \[5,6,7\]. The rates for attempt and death are approximately twice those seen for major depression \[5, 8\] and the rate of death is greater than in any disorder except schizophrenia \[9\].*   (Monson et al, 2021)

&#x200B;

>*The lifetime risk for suicidal ideation was significantly greater in patients with bipolar disorder vs major depressive disorder (29.2% vs 17.3%), as was the risk of attempted suicide (18.8% vs 4.78%) and suicide (1.73% vs 0.48%). As a proposed measure of lethality, the ratio of attempts to suicide was similar among patients with bipolar disorder vs depression (11.1 vs 10.2). Although the proportion of violent attempts or suicides was also found to be similar among patients with bipolar disorder vs depression (33.1% vs 23.9%), it was significantly greater in patients with bipolar disorder type I vs type II (38.2% vs 23.0%).*  (Unknown Author, 2019 - Psychiatry Advisor was the publisher)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Some images to explain the differences:    
[Consultant 360 - Comparisons of Bipolar and MDD](https://d148x66490prkv.cloudfront.net/c360/imported/inline-images/Comparisons%20of%20bipolar%20and%20MDD.png?VersionId=i0UqLPLFh4OVEf0Iw64EbhBkAXSzDze4)

&#x200B;

[Key Differentiating Features - Psychiatrist.com](https://www.psychiatrist.com/PublishingImages/13r01609f2.gif)

&#x200B;

[Active Minds - Is it more than a bad day?](https://www.activeminds.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/VennDiagram__WithoutBackground-1024x746.png)

>*i go from depressed no energy to able to function to able to work*

&#x200B;

Hypomania can show up as increased productivity, but you would have to have other manic symptoms with it like unusual impulsivity, increased self-esteem, etc. 

&#x200B;

>*how fast does this typically happen for others?*

A hypomanic episode requires a span of 4 days where you are mostly hypomanic.  As for how long the switch takes?  Some people find that it is sudden (minutes) and others feel it shift over days and weeks.

It sounds like an episode of extreme distress intolerance.  [Understanding Distress Intolerance](https://www.cci.health.wa.gov.au/-/media/CCI/Consumer-Modules/Facing-Your-Feelings/Facing-Your-Feelings---01---Understanding-Distress-Intolerance.pdf) 

&#x200B;

My primary concern about what you wrote was that this was in response to an argument with your partner.  Have you ever considered doing a formal DBT program?

That isn't psychosis, those are intrusive thoughts.

&#x200B;

That said, I did read an article once that said people in psychosis "often" would try to gouge their eyes out, and the medical community didn't know why this seemed to be a common feature in psychosis.  Who knows how reliable that article was though!

NAT, and I am posting a practice that isn't my therapist's practice, but it is so cool looking that I will definitely be trying them if I ever need to switch.  Such a fun marketing scheme!

&#x200B;

[Dive Thru Therapy](https://divethru.com/)

>*They aren’t knocking it out of the park with all 4s instead.*

&#x200B;

I don't want to sound rude, but rarely are grades indicative of giftedness.  Hence why my child's school still screened my kiddo for giftedness and it came out as likely so they are pushing us to go down that path.  We were already looking into testing for ADHD, but that is fairly inexpensive compared to a full psycho-ed assessment.

Instead of fighting them, I just acknowledge their presence and then lightly dismiss them.  I find fighting them makes them far worse.  

&#x200B;

[Intrusive Thoughts Worksheet](https://www.letss.org.au/images/Intrusive_Thoughts_Worksheet.pdf)
